each year on December 10th worshipers  convene in Scandinavia to commemorate  the passing of an international arms  dealer once known as the merchant of  death Alfred Nobel and doubt a prize for  the ages a way of rewarding and  encouraging scientists to make  discoveries for the benefit of mankind  300 years before the first Nobel Prize  Galileo Galilei opened up a new window  on the cosmos when he first turned a  small telescope up to the sky Galileo's  observations shook up the world  overthrowing the paradigm that the earth  was the center of the universe but  brilliant as he was even Galileo had his  own share of astronomical blunders he  misunderstood the reasons why the ocean  has tides and even claimed the Pleiades  were a pure assembly of stars and stars  alone when in reality dust makes up much  of this beautiful cluster recently the  LIGO experiment has opened a window into  the cosmos every bit as revolutionary as  Galileo's I go is the first of a new  kind of telescope a telescope that  listens to the cosmos in September 2015  LIGO detected the ripples in space-time  from the merger of two black holes these  gravitational waves that reversed the  cosmos were more than million years this  detection earned Nobel prizes at least  for three of Lycos leaders for the first  time astronomers hadn't merely seen  something using visible light but rather  gravitational waves minuscule ripples in  space-time resulting from some of the  biggest fireworks in the cosmos black  holes are invisible that's how they got  their name after all so to the beginning  of time is also invisible to our  instead the oldest light that  astronomers can use to study the cosmos  is called the Cosmic Microwave  Background the CMB the CMB is a constant  glow of microwaves that envelop us and  is the leftover thermal heat from when  the universe was a mere 380,000 years  old embedded within this cosmic glow  mail-id the imprint of something much  older primordial waves of gravity from  inflation which is theorized to occur  just a split second after the Big Bang  years before the LIGO discovery  scientists speculated that the earth of  the universe would be like witnessing a  sort of black hole in Reverse with all  the mass the entire universe bursting  forth in a microscopic flash of time  this violent eruption would have  released a tremendous amount of  gravitational wave energy and these  waves could still be visible today in  2005 my colleagues and I set out to  detect these disturbances in the fabric  of space-time by observing their imprint  on the ancient Cosmic Microwave  Background we did so from the coldest  darkest place on earth the South Pole  Antarctica using the bicep2 telescope  then in 2014 after observing for nearly  a decade the bicep2 team shocked the  world with our claim that we had  detected these long sought-after signals  that we had witnessed the spark that  ignited the Big Bang the world erupted  in applause and whispers of a nobel  prize abounded as well but there are  other forms of signals imposters that  can mimic the signals from inflation  these signals come from dust clouds not  in the cosmos but in our own Milky Way  galaxy these dusty mirages have vexed  astronomers for centuries and indeed  cause Galileo to make one of his very  few mistakes soon after our announcement  rumors began to spread that bicep2 had  also been mistaken who is right had we  seen the birth of the universe or had we  liked Galileo in fooled merely seeing  what we wanted to see what role does the  Nobel Prize play in the motivations of  scientists today how does the Nobel  Prize fail to live up to  Alfred's lofty vision  losing the nobel prize tells my  insider's account of what it was like to  be a part of one of the most exciting  and controversial scientific discoveries  of the modern era
 

